Fifth session, on the car, driven remotely. The one that found the shape of
the fault.

Context that arrived with it: the owner reports other readers DO work on this
car, so the socket and the diagnostic bus are known good, and the cable is new
and undamaged.

=== CBUS SELF-CHECK =========================================================
Prompted by the EEPROM: bytes 0x14/0x15 read 0xAA, and 0xA is the FT232R code
for IOMODE, so CBUS0-3 are meant to be driven by host software. A plain serial
adapter never needs that, so the obvious guess was a transceiver enable.

  EEPROM CBUS config:
    CBUS0 = IOMODE (software-driven GPIO)
    CBUS1 = IOMODE (software-driven GPIO)
    CBUS2 = IOMODE (software-driven GPIO)
    CBUS3 = IOMODE (software-driven GPIO)
    CBUS4 = CLK48

  set 0000  read 0000  ok      set 1000  read 1000  ok
  set 0001  read 0001  ok      set 1001  read 1001  ok
  set 0010  read 0010  ok      set 1010  read 1010  ok
  set 0011  read 0011  ok      set 1011  read 1011  ok
  set 0100  read 0100  ok      set 1100  read 1100  ok
  set 0101  read 0101  ok      set 1101  read 1101  ok
  set 0110  read 0110  ok      set 1110  read 1110  ok
  set 0111  read 0111  ok      set 1111  read 1111  ok
  16 of 16 values read back as written

So CBUS control works. The preceding hunt - all 16 states x 2 bauds x 2
parities x 8 addresses - produced no valid frame and byte counts that barely
moved (37-38 at 9600, 38-42 at 10400). That negative is therefore real: these
pins do not gate the bus on this cable.

=== SPACED BYTE SEND ========================================================
Prompted by the vendor's own bmwscan.ini: Delay=20, Delay2=40 under
[Communication]. Every transmission so far had been one back-to-back burst.

  gap= 5ms addr=0x00 TX 00 04 00 04  RX C4 F8 59 FF
  gap= 5ms addr=0x12 TX 12 04 00 16  RX C4 F8 FE 19 FF
  gap= 5ms addr=0x60 TX 60 04 00 64  RX C4 98 7D FE FF
  gap= 5ms addr=0x80 TX 80 04 00 84  RX C4 D8 9B 1F
  gap=20ms addr=0x00 TX 00 04 00 04  RX C4 88 E1 FE
  gap=20ms addr=0x12 TX 12 04 00 16  RX C4 28 81 C2 FF
  gap=20ms addr=0x60 TX 60 04 00 64  RX C4 08 D4 F3 FF
  gap=20ms addr=0x80 TX 80 04 00 84  RX C4 08 63 87
  gap=40ms addr=0x00 TX 00 04 00 04  RX C4 58 FF FE
  gap=40ms addr=0x12 TX 12 04 00 16  RX C4 D8 9C 50 FE
  gap=40ms addr=0x60 TX 60 04 00 64  RX C4 F8 3E 11 F8
  gap=40ms addr=0x80 TX 80 04 00 84  RX C4 D8 39 ED
  gap=80ms addr=0x00 TX 00 04 00 04  RX C4 88 C4 58 C4 28 C4 98
  gap=80ms addr=0x12 TX 12 04 00 16  RX C4 08 FD C4 28 C4 F8 C4 C8 FF
  gap=80ms addr=0x60 TX 60 04 00 64  RX C4 68 F8 C4 28 C4 F8 C4 C8 FC
  gap=80ms addr=0x80 TX 80 04 00 84  RX C4 88 C4 58 C4 A8 C4 98 FA

At 80 ms the structure separates: every transmitted byte produces exactly two
received bytes, C4 followed by one more. Packed back-to-back these artifacts
interleave, which is the mush every earlier run recorded.

And the artifacts are largely independent of the data. Two completely
different frames:

  TX 00 04 00 04  ->  C4 88  C4 58  C4 28  C4 98
  TX 80 04 00 84  ->  C4 88  C4 58  C4 A8  C4 98

A working K-line echo would carry our bytes back. This carries a fixed shape
triggered once per transmitted byte.
